<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 45 M5, AC5.2 / AC5.5 / D6: THE EIGHT VERBS THAT MUST REFUSE A SYSTEM
  LANE, EACH WITH ITS OWN MESSAGE, EACH LEAVING THE MODEL UNTOUCHED.

    arm-track                 sliveinputactions.cpp
    set-monitor-mode          sliveinputactions.cpp
    set-track-input           sliveinputactions.cpp
    set-track-solo            ssettracksoloaction.cpp
    set-track-midi-output     ssettrackmidioutputaction.cpp
    remove-track              sremovetrackaction.cpp
    move-track                smovetrackaction.cpp
    reparent-track            sreparenttrackaction.cpp

  WHY EACH ONE, in the proposal's own terms:

    THE THREE LIVE-INPUT VERBS are one refusal in one shared resolver, and
    refusing only arm-track would not be enough. The live set is
    `{armed && monitorEffective} U {monitor == on}`, so a master left on
    monitor "on" is live-owned whether it was ever armed or not, and a
    live-owned master is exactly the shape D4a refuses to render. Two doors,
    one contradiction.

    SOLO IS NOT MERELY USELESS ON A MASTER, IT IS UNREADABLE. A master lane is
    deliberately not a childLinks() member (D2), and ssolorules::anySoloInTree
    walks exactly those children - so a solo flag set here is state that no
    audibility rule ever consults. That is the SStdMixer::volume_ defect in a
    new field: a control that appears to work and changes nothing. MUTE is the
    opposite case and is deliberately ACCEPTED; master_mute_audible is where
    that half lives.

    MIDI OUT has nothing to send. STrack::eventFeed() merges a lane's OWN clip
    set with the children that bubble up, and a system lane carries no clips
    (D6), so a port set here would be opened, chased and panicked for a feed
    that is empty by construction.

    THE THREE STRUCTURAL VERBS are "it is not the user's object". They also
    happen to be the three where the refusal was NOT free: each addresses a
    track as "its parent, plus its index in that parent", and because the
    master is not a child link, `$master` splits into an empty parent path and
    the index -1, childAt(-1) answers null, and every one of them ALREADY
    refused - silently, by an accident of the sentinel's numeric value. That
    accident stops holding the moment M7 gives a send lane a name form or M6
    nests a conductor lane one level down. Asking explicitly is what makes it
    a policy rather than a coincidence, and it is what announces it.

  AC5.5 IS WHAT THE SECOND HALF OF THIS CASE IS FOR, and expectReject alone
  cannot supply it: a verb that wrote its field and THEN refused would return
  false just the same. assert-system-lane grew six observables for this
  (armed / monitor / input / solo / muted / midiOutPort), so every refusal is
  followed by the state it would have written if it had half-applied.

  NOT ASSERTED HERE, and named rather than left implied: whether a USER track
  may be reparented INTO the master. AC5.2 asks about the ADDRESSED object and
  this case asks the same question; D3 gives the master lane child tracks, so a
  destination refusal would be a design decision rather than a policy one.
  reparent-track checks its SOURCE only.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="master_refuses_arm_and_structure">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>

    <!-- THE BASELINE. Every field the eight verbs could write, read off the
         master before any of them is asked, so the "untouched" assertions
         below are compared against a state this case established rather than
         against a default nobody checked. -->
    <assert-system-lane trackPath="$master" role="master" inChildLinks="0"
                        armed="0" monitor="auto" input="none" solo="0"
                        muted="0" midiOutPort="&lt;none&gt;"/>

    <!-- ================= THE LIVE-INPUT THREE ================= -->

    <arm-track trackPath="$master" armed="1" expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-log level="warn" minCount="1"
                contains="arm-track: refused on the master lane"/>
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="$master" mode="on" expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-log level="warn" minCount="1"
                contains="set-monitor-mode: refused on the master lane"/>
    <set-track-input trackPath="$master" input="audio:file:3"
                     expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-log level="warn" minCount="1"
                contains="set-track-input: refused on the master lane"/>

    <!-- ...and none of the three left a trace. If arm-track had set the flag
         before refusing, or set-track-input had stored the string, this is
         where it shows. -->
    <assert-system-lane trackPath="$master" armed="0" monitor="auto"
                        input="none"/>

    <!-- set-monitor-mode is refused for EVERY mode, not only "on". There is
         nothing to express by setting Auto or Off on a lane that can never
         monitor, and accepting two of three would put a value in the model
         that no reader may act on. -->
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="$master" mode="off" expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-system-lane trackPath="$master" monitor="auto"/>

    <!-- ================= SOLO AND MIDI OUT ================= -->

    <set-track-solo trackPath="$master" solo="1" expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-log level="warn" minCount="1"
                contains="set-track-solo: refused on the master lane"/>
    <set-track-midi-output trackPath="$master" port="Capture MIDI Out"
                           expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-log level="warn" minCount="1"
                contains="set-track-midi-output: refused on the master lane"/>
    <assert-system-lane trackPath="$master" solo="0"
                        midiOutPort="&lt;none&gt;"/>

    <!-- ================= THE STRUCTURAL THREE ================= -->

    <!-- FOR THESE THREE THE LOG ASSERTION IS THE ONLY THING THAT BITES, and
         that was MEASURED, not assumed. Removing each explicit check and
         re-running leaves this case GREEN on the expectReject and
         assert-system-lane assertions alone: the verbs still refuse, through
         the childAt(-1) accident described in their own source. What the
         accident does NOT do is ANNOUNCE, and D6 requires the announcement
         ("a bound is ANNOUNCED, never silent"). So the property these three
         assertions gate is the message, and each was watched failing by
         deleting its own refusal. -->

    <remove-track trackPath="$master" expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-log level="warn" minCount="1"
                contains="remove-track: refused on the master lane"/>
    <move-track source="$master" toIndex="0" expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-log level="warn" minCount="1"
                contains="move-track: refused on the master lane"/>
    <reparent-track source="$master" destParent="0" expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-log level="warn" minCount="1"
                contains="reparent-track: refused on the master lane"/>

    <!-- The lane is still there, still the master, still outside childLinks,
         and the user tracks are still two and still where they were. A
         reparent that half-applied would have detached the lane; a move that
         half-applied would have put it INTO the child list, which is the one
         thing mixer inv. 1 says can never happen. -->
    <assert-system-lane trackPath="$master" role="master" inChildLinks="0"
                        expectPath="$master"/>
    <assert-track-count count="2"/>

    <!-- ================= THE CONTROL ================= -->
    <!-- Every one of the eight still works on an ORDINARY track, so what the
         refusals above measure is the POLICY and not eight verbs that stopped
         resolving anything. Without this half the case would pass just as well
         against a build in which all eight were broken outright. -->

    <arm-track trackPath="1" armed="1"/>
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="1" mode="on"/>
    <set-track-input trackPath="1" input="audio:file:3"/>
    <set-track-solo trackPath="1" solo="1"/>
    <set-track-midi-output trackPath="1" port="Capture MIDI Out"/>
    <assert-system-lane trackPath="1" role="none" armed="1" monitor="on"
                        input="audio:file:3" solo="1"
                        midiOutPort="Capture MIDI Out"/>

    <!-- Put track 1 back so the structural control below is not measuring an
         armed, soloed, monitored lane. -->
    <set-track-solo trackPath="1" solo="0"/>
    <arm-track trackPath="1" armed="0"/>
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="1" mode="auto"/>

    <move-track source="1" toIndex="0"/>
    <assert-track-count count="2"/>
    <reparent-track source="1" destParent="0"/>
    <assert-track-count count="1"/>
    <remove-track trackPath="0,0"/>
    <assert-track-count count="1"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
